{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790458428,"nonce":"QsnorKsIrCzjxO0TJ8JFFWQR8lk=","message":"Correction (user, 2026-09-26): Ash's browser runtime is not only a single-agent fiber transform. On wasm32-wasip1-threads, krio (krio-parallel, krio-wasm; ash docs/internals/wasm-threads.md) runs fibers and tasks on real Web Workers over one shared memory. A fiber parks on memory.atomic.wait32/notify, with no host involved; work is stolen across agents; stop-the-world safepoints and an epoch clock live in shared memory. The ASH_WASM_FIBERS transform is the single-agent fallback for a page without COOP/COEP.\nThis reshapes gaps 3 to 5. As Ash's canvas.rs says, the agent running the program never returns to its event loop, so it cannot see a Promise resolve or present a frame. WebGPU must live on an agent that does, a GPU worker (holding the GPUDevice and an OffscreenCanvas) or the page, driven remotely through shared memory as Ash's canvas already is:\n- The WebIDL-generated binding is a command encoder and decoder pair. The plugin side writes each WebGPU operation (interface, operation, handle, marshalled arguments, by the WebIDL) into a queue in shared memory. The GPU agent's generated JS decodes and executes it against its own objects, keyed by handles in a table on that agent.\n- Results and Promises come back through shared slots. The calling fiber parks on its slot with atomic.wait, and the GPU agent notifies when the operation or the Promise completes. So requestAdapter, requestDevice and mapAsync block the calling fiber while its worker's other fibers keep running.\n- Buffers and textures written from the program (writeBuffer, writeTexture, mapped ranges) are addresses in shared memory, read by the GPU agent without copying out of the program.\n- Presenting is the GPU agent's: getCurrentTexture and present on its own clock.\nNew gap: caribou's runtime object is built for wasm32-wasip1 only. This needs the -threads variant, and caribou's scheduler on krio's wasm agents (krio-wasm set_spawn and agents) rather than the single-agent path. Caribou's heap already compiles for wasm32-wasip1-threads, with its parallel marker as dead code there.","files":null}]}